00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,560
Hi.

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,880
In this video, we'll look at testing for DOM XSS with DOM Invader.

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:17,200
DOM-based XSS, or DOM XSS, arises when an application contains client-side JavaScript

4
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,920
that processes data from an untrusted source in an unsafe way, usually by writing the data

5
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,000
back to the DOM.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,280
DOM Invader makes it much easier for you to test applications for DOM XSS.

7
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:34,460
It injects a unique string into different sources, and then shows you the syncs that your input

8
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:36,360
flows into.

9
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,320
It also shows you the surrounding context.

10
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:42,920
This replaces the need to manually follow the flow through complex JavaScript, which could

11
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:45,960
have thousands of lines of code.

12
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:49,620
You can learn more about sources and syncs in the DOM-based vulnerabilities section on our

13
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:51,120
Web Security Academy.

14
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:57,400
If you'd like to follow along with these steps, you can use the lab DOM XSS in document.write

15
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:04,880
sync, using source location.search, which is also available in our Web Security Academy.

16
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:08,300
DOM Invader is pre-installed in Burps Browser.

17
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:13,620
It's disabled by default, as some of its features may interfere with your other testing activities.

18
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:18,180
So before we start, we'll need to enable DOM Invader.

19
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:21,940
Use Burps Browser to visit your target website.

20
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:26,560
Right click the browser window and select Inspect.

21
